#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# full_simplify

#% text
# `full_simplify(e)` gives the simplest form of `e` it finds by a wider search than `simplify`: special functions are also expanded and identities between them tried.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The search is `simplify`'s, with `function_expand` as one more rewriting, every subexpression searched on its own before the expression it is in, and more steps.

#% item
# Identities of special functions are used when they make the form simpler: the reflection and recurrence of gamma and polygamma, the dilogarithm reflection, reductions of hypergeometric functions to named functions, Bessel functions, sums of arctangents of rationals, the inverse of `product_log`.

#% item
# An identity guarded by a condition (`product_log(x*exp(x))` is `x` only for `x >= -1`) is used only when the assumptions prove the condition.

#% item
# Every form found by an identity is checked numerically at a few points (satisfying the assumptions) before it is returned; a form that differs from the input is not used.

#% item
# Among equally simple forms the one written with an identity's named function is preferred (`full_simplify(x*gamma(x))` is `gamma(1 + x)`).

#% item
# Options: `assumptions: a`, `complexity_function: f` as for `simplify`; `transformation_functions: [automatic, f, ...]` adds the functions `f` as rewritings (without `automatic`, only they are used); `excluded_forms: [p, ...]` leaves subexpressions matching the patterns as they are; `time_constraint: s` limits the time in seconds; `trig: false` leaves out the trigonometric factoring.

#% item
# `full_simplify(e, facts)` simplifies under the extra facts.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The gamma recurrence:

#% cell
full_simplify(x*gamma(x))
#% output
#= gamma(1 + x)

#% text
# Machin's formula:

#% cell
full_simplify(16*atan(1/5) - 4*atan(1/239))
#% output
#= pi

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The dilogarithm reflection:

#% cell
full_simplify(polylog(2, 1 - z) + polylog(2, z) - zeta(2))
#% output
#= -log(z)*log(1 - z)

#% text
# An inverse function under a condition:

#% cell
full_simplify(product_log(x*log(x)), x > 1/e)
#% output
#= log(x)

#% text
# Modified Bessel functions:

#% cell
full_simplify(csc(pi*v)*(bessel_i(-v, z) - bessel_i(v, z))/2)
#% output
#= bessel_k(v, z)/pi

#% text
# Gamma and error functions, and inverse trigonometric functions:

#% cell
full_simplify(gamma(x + 1)/gamma(x))
#% output
#= x

#% cell
full_simplify(erf(x) + erfc(x))
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
full_simplify(asin(x) + acos(x))
#% output
#= (1/2)*pi

#% cell
full_simplify(atan(1/2) + atan(1/3))
#% output
#= (1/4)*pi

#% text
# Binomial coefficients, and index shifts of the Fibonacci numbers for integers:

#% cell
full_simplify(binomial(n, 2)*2 - n*(n - 1))
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
full_simplify(fibonacci(n + 2) - fibonacci(n + 1) - fibonacci(n), element(n, integers))
#% output
#= 0

#% text
# Square roots of products need the signs:

#% cell
full_simplify(sqrt(x)*sqrt(y) - sqrt(x*y), x > 0 and y > 0)
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# Leave the logarithm alone:

#% cell
full_simplify(im_part(log(x) + 2*x - 3*y) - 2*im_part(x), excluded_forms: [log(_)])
#% output
#= im_part(log(x) - 3*y)

#% text
# `assumptions:` as an option, or the facts as a second argument:

#% cell
full_simplify(sqrt(x^2), assumptions: x > 0)
#% output
#= x

#% cell
full_simplify(sqrt(x^2), x < 0)
#% output
#= -x

#% text
# An identity with a condition that the assumptions prove, and without them:

#% cell
full_simplify(product_log(x*exp(x)), x >= -1)
#% output
#= x

#% cell
full_simplify(product_log(x*exp(x)))
#% output
#= product_log(x*exp(x))

#% text
# `trig: false` leaves out the trigonometric factoring, and `transformation_functions:` without `automatic` uses only the given functions:

#% cell
full_simplify(sin(x)*cos(x), trig: false)
#% output
#= cos(x)*sin(x)

#% cell
full_simplify(sin(x)^2 + cos(x)^2, transformation_functions: [expand])
#% output
#= cos(x)^2 + sin(x)^2

#% text
# `time_constraint:` in seconds; a very short limit still gives the identity that comes first:

#% cell
full_simplify(x*gamma(x), time_constraint: 0.0001)
#% output
#= gamma(1 + x)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# A form that is not simpler is not taken:

#% cell
[simplify(chebyshev_t(n, x)), full_simplify(chebyshev_t(n, x))]
#% output
#= [chebyshev_t(n, x), chebyshev_t(n, x)]

#% text
# The result has the same value as the input at a sample point:

#% cell
[(asin(x) + acos(x)) /. x -> 0.3, full_simplify(asin(x) + acos(x)) /. x -> 0.3]
#% output
#= [1.5707963267948968, (1/2)*pi]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Some identities are not found: a hypergeometric function that is a logarithm, and a shift by a multiple of `pi` with an integer:

#% cell
full_simplify(hypergeometric_2f1(1, 1, 2, -x))
#% output
#= log(1 + x)/x

#% cell
full_simplify(sin(n*pi + x), element(n, integers))
#% output
#= (-1)^n*sin(x)

#% text
# A condition that is not known blocks a rewriting: `log(exp(x))` needs `x` real:

#% cell
full_simplify(log(exp(x)))
#% output
#= log(exp(x))

#% cell
full_simplify(log(exp(x)), element(x, reals))
#% output
#= x

#% text
# Reductions of special functions that `simplify` leaves alone:

#% cell
simplify(hypergeometric2f1(1, 1, 2, -x))
#% output
#= log(1 + x)/x

#% cell
full_simplify(hypergeometric2f1(1, 1, 2, -x))
#% output
#= log(1 + x)/x

#% cell
full_simplify(exp_integral_e(1, x) - gamma(0, x), x > 0)
#% output
#= 0

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `simplify`, `function_expand`, `refine`, `assuming`
